From e3d53ec0be92a9bbca3b2fe8be0ffd73f8a8673b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: William Emerison Six <billsix@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 03:25:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 355/488] soh: name the 18 address-named symbols defined in
 z_en_test.c

SoH carries the Ocarina of Time decomp, in which several thousand symbols are
still named after their ROM address. This commit names every such symbol DEFINED
IN ONE FILE, so the work can be reviewed a file at a time.

The file names the unit; it does not bound it. Each rename is applied wherever
the symbol occurs -- the declaration in the header, the definition, and every
call site -- so this commit reaches into whatever other files use these symbols
and leaves no dangling reference. It is a pure rename: no behaviour changes, and
the only edits that are not renames are the provenance comments described below.

Two name sources are used, and every name says which it came from:

  * zeldaret/oot -- SoH's decomp IS zeldaret/oot at the same ROM addresses, so
    where oot has already named a symbol, that name is adopted verbatim. The
    definition gets a "Name from zeldaret/oot" comment and every declaration a
    short "// was <addr> [oot]" tag.

  * DEDUCED -- oot leaves a great many symbols address-named too, so those
    names are inferred from the body and its callers, and are marked as OURS
    rather than passed off as upstream. The definition gets
    "LLM generated name (HIGH|GUESS), was <addr>: <reason>" and the short tag
    reads "[LLM:HIGH]" or "[LLM:GUESS]". A GUESS is tagged at every call site
    as well, not only the declaration, so a reader who meets the name anywhere
    knows it is inferred. "git grep '[LLM:'" lists every place the decomp
    leans on an inference.

The justification recorded for each individual name follows, one entry per
symbol, in the order the renames were made. Nothing is summarised away: these
are the per-commit reasons from the unsquashed history, with the boilerplate
that was identical in every one of them hoisted into the text above.

All 18 are defined in soh/src/overlays/actors/ovl_En_Test/z_en_test.c.

  * func_80860BDC -> EnTest_SetupTurnToPlayer
    deduced, confidence HIGH: loops gStalfosSidestepAnim and hands off to
    the action that swings shape.rot.y onto yawTowardsPlayer with
    NA_SE_EN_STAL_WALK footsteps

  * func_80860C24 -> EnTest_TurnToPlayer
    deduced, confidence HIGH: rotates shape.rot.y toward yawTowardsPlayer,
    scaling anim playSpeed with the turn rate, until Actor_IsFacingPlayer

  * func_80860EC0 -> EnTest_SetupSidestep
    deduced, confidence GUESS: same gStalfosSidestepAnim as the circling
    pair; distinguished only by starting from rest at +/-0.5 speed and by
    being the variant taken when another actor is targeted

  * func_80860F84 -> EnTest_Sidestep
    deduced, confidence GUESS: strafes at shape.rot.y + 0x3E80 while
    smooth-turning to face the player, bouncing off walls; the untargeted
    counterpart of EnTest_CircleAroundPlayer

  * func_80862154 -> EnTest_SetupFlinchFront
    deduced, confidence HIGH: plays gStalfosFlinchFromHitFrontAnim with
    NA_SE_EN_STAL_DAMAGE and a knockback of -2

  * func_808621D4 -> EnTest_FlinchFront
    deduced, confidence HIGH: decays the knockback, then blocks, jumps back
    or jumps up depending on the wall and the player's swing

  * func_80862398 -> EnTest_SetupFlinchBehind
    deduced, confidence HIGH: plays gStalfosFlinchFromHitBehindAnim with
    NA_SE_EN_STAL_DAMAGE and a knockback of -2

  * func_80862418 -> EnTest_FlinchBehind
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the behind-hit twin of EnTest_FlinchFront,
    minus its jump-up-at-a-wall escape

  * func_808627C4 -> EnTest_SetupCircleAroundPlayer
    deduced, confidence HIGH: morphs to gStalfosSidestepAnim at +/-4 speed
    with world.rot.y a quarter-turn off shape.rot.y -- the AI's usual dodge

  * func_808628C8 -> EnTest_CircleAroundPlayer
    deduced, confidence HIGH: strafes sideways around the player, reversing
    off walls and off missing floor for the ceiling type

  * func_80862DBC -> EnTest_SetupBreakApart
    deduced, confidence HIGH: allocates the BodyBreak parts, drops targeting
    and the sword blur, and hands off to the action that scatters them

  * func_80862E6C -> EnTest_BreakApart
    deduced, confidence HIGH: spawns the BodyBreak parts, then either
    reassembles at 10 health or drops its collectible and dies

  * func_80862FA8 -> EnTest_SetupDeathFallBackwards
    deduced, confidence HIGH: plays gStalfosFallOverBackwardsAnim with
    NA_SE_EN_STAL_DEAD; the higher params fall through to
    EnTest_SetupBreakApart instead

  * func_80863044 -> EnTest_DeathFallBackwards
    deduced, confidence HIGH: waits out the backwards death animation,
    thumping at frame 15, then allocates the body break

  * func_808630F0 -> EnTest_SetupDeathFallForwards
    deduced, confidence HIGH: plays gStalfosFallOverForwardsAnim with
    NA_SE_EN_STAL_DEAD; the forwards twin of EnTest_SetupDeathFallBackwards

  * func_8086318C -> EnTest_DeathFallForwards
    deduced, confidence HIGH: waits out the forwards death animation,
    thumping at frames 10 and 25, then allocates the body break

  * func_808633E8 -> EnTest_DeathBodyBreak
    deduced, confidence HIGH: scatters the body-break parts, drops a random
    collectible, decrements the parent's count and kills the actor

  * func_80864158 -> EnTest_SetupCircleAroundPlayerWithSpeed
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the same circling setup as
    EnTest_SetupCircleAroundPlayer but taking the strafe speed from the
    caller

